{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Implementing an Autoencoder\n",
    "\n",
    "In this exercise, we would like to train on a popular face dataset, a sparse auto-encoder. We consider the simple two-layer autoencoder network:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{align*}\n",
    "\\boldsymbol{z}_i &= \\max(0,V \\boldsymbol{x}_i + \\boldsymbol{b}) & \\text{(layer 1)}\\\\\n",
    "\\hat{\\boldsymbol{x}}_i &= W \\boldsymbol{z}_i + \\boldsymbol{a} & \\text{(layer 2)}\n",
    "\\end{align*}\n",
    "\n",
    "where $W,V$ are matrices of parameters of the encoder and the decoder, and $\\boldsymbol{b},\\boldsymbol{a}$ are additional bias parameters. We seek to maximize the objective:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{align*}\n",
    "\\min_{W} ~~\n",
    "\\underbrace{ \\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N \\| \\boldsymbol{x}_i - \\hat{\\boldsymbol{x}}_i \\|^2}_{\\text{reconstruction}}\n",
    "+ \\lambda \\cdot\n",
    "\\underbrace{\\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N \\|\\boldsymbol{z}_i\\|_1}_{\\text{sparsity}}\n",
    "+ \\epsilon \\cdot\n",
    "\\underbrace{\\sum_{j=1}^h\\Big( \\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N [\\boldsymbol{z}_i]_j \\Big)^{-1}}_{\\text{\"entropy\"}}\n",
    "+ \\eta \\cdot \\!\\!\\!\\!\n",
    "\\underbrace{\\phantom{\\Bigg[}\\|W\\|_F^2 \\phantom{\\Bigg[}}_{\\text{regularization}}\n",
    "\\end{align*}\n",
    "\n",
    "The objective is composed of four terms: The reconstruction term is the standard mean square error between the data points and their reconstructions. The sparsity term applies a l1-norm to drive activation to zero in the representation. The \"entropy\" term that ensures that at least a few examples activate each source dimension. The regularization term ensures that the sparsity term remains effective."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Loading the dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "We first load the Labeled Faces in the Wild (LFW) dataset. The LFW is a popular face recognition dataset which is readily available from scikit learn. When loading the dataset, we specify some image downscaling in order to limit the computation resources. The following code visualizes a few images that we have extracted from the LFW dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.080781Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.080540Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.952855Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.952340Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x250 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import sklearn,sklearn.datasets\n",
    "import matplotlib\n",
    "%matplotlib inline\n",
    "from matplotlib import pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "data = sklearn.datasets.fetch_lfw_people(resize=0.3)['images']\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12,2.5))\n",
    "plt.imshow(data[:32].reshape(2,16,37,28).transpose(0,2,1,3).reshape(2*37,16*28),cmap='gray')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Implementing the autoencoder (20 P)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We now would like to train an autoencoder on this data. As a first step, we standardize the data, which is a usual step before training a ML model. (Note that contrarily to other component analyses such as ICA, the data does not need to be whitened.)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.970216Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.970062Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.992252Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.991890Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "X = data.reshape(len(data),-1)\n",
    "X = X - X.mean(axis=0)\n",
    "X = X / X.std()\n",
    "print(X)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To learn the autoencoder, we need to optimize the objective function above. This can be done using by gradient descent, or some enhanced gradient-based optimizer such as Adam. Because a manual computation of the gradients can be difficult and error-prone, we will make use of automatic differentiation readily provided by the PyTorch software. PyTorch uses its own structures for storing the data and the model parameters. (You can consult the tutorials at https://pytorch.org/tutorials/ to learn the basics.)\n",
    "\n",
    "We first convert the data into a PyTorch tensor."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.993834Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-04-08T14:57:33.993749Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.498634Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.498247Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import torch\n",
    "\n",
    "X  = torch.FloatTensor(X)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Recall that the four terms that compose the objective function are given by:\n",
    "\\begin{align*}\n",
    "\\text{rec} &= \\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N \\| \\boldsymbol{x}_i - \\hat{\\boldsymbol{x}}_i \\|^2 &\n",
    "\\text{spa} &= \\Big(\\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N \\|\\boldsymbol{z}_i\\|_1\\Big)\\\\\n",
    "\\text{ent} &= \\sum_{j=1}^h\\Big( \\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N [\\boldsymbol{z}_i]_j \\Big)^{-1} &\n",
    "\\text{reg} &= \\|W\\|_F^2\n",
    "\\end{align*}\n",
    "\n",
    "**Task:**\n",
    "\n",
    " * **Create the function `get_objective_terms` that computes these terms.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The function receives as input:\n",
    "\n",
    " * A `FloatTensor` `X` of size $m \\times d$ containing a data minibatch of $m$ examples.\n",
    " * A `FloatTensor` `V` of size $d \\times h$ containing the weights of the encoder.\n",
    " * A `FloatTensor` `W` of size $h \\times d$ containing the weights of the decoder.\n",
    " * A `FloatTensor` `b` of size $h$ containing the bias of the encoder.\n",
    " * A `FloatTensor` `a` of size $d$ containing the bias of the decoder.\n",
    " \n",
    "In your function, the parameter $\\epsilon$ can be hardcoded to 0.01. The function should return the four terms (`rec`, `spa`, `ent`, `reg`) of the objective. (These terms will be merged later on in a single objective function.) While implementing the `get_objective_terms` function, make sure to use PyTorch functions so that the gradient information necessary for automatic differentiation is retained. For example, converting arrays to numpy will not work as this will remove the gradient information."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.500140Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.500059Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.501958Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.501596Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def get_objective_terms(X,V,W,b,a):\n",
    "    # -----------------------------------------------------\n",
    "    # TODO: replace by your code\n",
    "    # -----------------------------------------------------\n",
    "    import solutions\n",
    "    rec,spa,ent,reg = solutions.get_objective_terms(X,V,W,b,a)\n",
    "    # -----------------------------------------------------\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return rec,spa,ent,reg"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Training the autoencoder\n",
    "\n",
    "Now that the terms of the objective function have been implemented, the model can be trained to minimize the objective. The code below calls the function `get_objective_terms` repeatedly (once per iteration). Automatic differentiation is used to compute the gradient, and we use Adam (a state-of-the-art optimizer for neural networks) to optimize the parameters. The number of units in the representation is hard-coded to $h=400$, and we use the parameter $\\eta=1$ for the regularizer."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.503138Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.503069Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.505624Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-04-08T14:57:34.505295Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import torch.optim\n",
    "import torch.nn\n",
    "import numpy\n",
    "\n",
    "def train(X,lambd=0):\n",
    "    \n",
    "    d = X.shape[1]\n",
    "    h = 400\n",
    "    \n",
    "    eps = 0.01 * lambd # hard-coded parameter\n",
    "    eta = 1 * lambd    # hard-coded parameter\n",
    "    \n",
    "    V = torch.nn.Parameter(d**-.5*torch.randn([d,h]))\n",
    "    W = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.zeros([h,d]))\n",
    "    b = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.zeros([h]))\n",
    "    a = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.zeros([d]))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    optimizer = torch.optim.Adam((V,W,b,a), lr=0.0001)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    print('%7s %8s %8s %8s %8s'%('nbit','rec','spa','ent','reg'))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    for i in range(0,10001):\n",
    "        \n",
    "        optimizer.zero_grad()\n",
    "        \n",
    "        x= X[numpy.random.permutation(len(X))[:100]]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        rec,spa,ent,reg = get_objective_terms(x,V,W,b,a)\n",
    "        \n",
    "        (rec + lambd*spa + eps*ent + eta*reg).backward()\n",
    "        \n",
    "        if i%1000 == 0: print('%7d %8.2f %8.2f %8.2f %8.2f'%(i,rec.data,spa.data,ent.data,reg.data))\n",
    "        \n",
    "        optimizer.step()\n",
    "\n",
    "    return V,W,b,a"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Dense Autoencoder\n",
    "\n",
    "We first train an autoencoder with parameter $\\lambda=0$, that is, a standard autoencoder without sparsity. The parameters of the learn autoencoder are stored in the variables `V`, `W`, `b`, `a`. Running the code may take a few minutes. You may temporarily reduce the number of iterations when testing your implementation."
   ]
  },
